"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Toy planet, {#} days a year; Toy planet, {#} days a year; Always faces the Sun; Earth; Mars; Mercury; Venus; Jupiter; The Moon; Earth: sundial and clock; The ellipse alone (no tilt); The tilt alone (circular orbit); A much more stretched orbit.",
"This worksheet uses two of the three switches at the top of Reasons for Seasons: Sidereal and solar days, and Sundial and clock.",
"<strong>Sidereal and solar days</strong> is a top view of a planet going round the Sun, seen from above its north pole. A person stands on the planet’s equator. A dashed line points toward a very distant star, always in the same direction; another points toward the Sun. The red arc shows how much farther the planet must still turn before the person faces the Sun again.",
"Play runs the planet; Next sidereal day runs it until the person faces the star again, and Next solar day until the person faces the Sun again (noon). The counters in the corner give the time and how many turns against the star and how many noons have passed. Restart sets the clock back to zero.",
"The toy planets spin once every {#} hours against the stars and take only a few of those turns to go round their Sun, so the effect is easy to see. Spin backward makes the planet turn the other way, like Venus. The close up shows the angle between the star and the Sun as seen from the planet.",
"<strong>Sundial and clock</strong> shows Earth through the year: how far a sundial runs ahead of or behind a clock (the equation of time), how long each solar day is, where the Sun stands at clock noon, and a sundial at Ruston’s latitude at {#}:{#} on the clock, with a clock face whose orange hand shows the time the sundial reads. Drag across a chart or use the date slider to change the date. The eccentricity (how stretched the orbit is) and the tilt of the axis can be changed.",
"In the top view the planets are drawn as seen from above their north pole, with Earth from NASA’s Blue Marble image. Sizes and distances in the top view are not to scale. Record reading stores the counters, or the date and readouts. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the worksheet; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of a table.",
"Find out why a day measured by the Sun is longer than one turn of a planet, and why a sundial does not keep the same time as a clock.",
"<strong>Two kinds of day.</strong> A planet’s <strong>sidereal day</strong> is the time it takes to turn once compared with the distant stars. Its <strong>solar day</strong> is the time from one noon to the next, when the Sun is highest in the sky. They are not the same, because while the planet turns it also moves along its orbit. After one full turn, the Sun is no longer in quite the same direction, so the planet has to turn a little farther to bring the Sun back overhead.",
"<strong>Counting through a year.</strong> In one orbit, a planet that spins the same way it orbits faces the stars one more time than it faces the Sun. If it turns N times against the stars in a year, it has N − {#} noons. So the solar day is the year divided by N − {#}, and the sidereal day is the year divided by N. Earth turns about {#} times against the stars in a year, so it has about {#} solar days.",
"<strong>Earth’s two days.</strong> Earth’s sidereal day is {#} hours {#} minutes {#} seconds. Earth moves about {#}° along its orbit each day ({#}° in about {#} days), and turning {#}° more takes about {#} minutes ({#} hours ÷ {#}). That makes the solar day {#} hours, which is what our clocks keep.",
"<strong>Sundials and clocks.</strong> A sundial reads noon when the Sun crosses the north and south line in the sky. A clock keeps an average day of exactly {#} hours. Because Earth’s orbit is an ellipse (Earth moves faster when it is closer to the Sun) and Earth’s axis is tilted, the Sun’s daily step against the stars is not the same all year. Some solar days are a little longer than {#} hours and some a little shorter, and the small differences add up, so a sundial can be up to about a quarter of an hour ahead of or behind a clock.",
"A planet turns {#} times against the stars in one orbit, in the same direction it orbits. How many noons does it have in that orbit? If its year is {#} hours long, how long is its sidereal day and how long is its solar day?",
"Earth moves about {#}° ÷ {#} days along its orbit in one day. Work out that angle, then the time Earth takes to turn that far if one full turn ({#}°) takes {#} hours. Give the answer in minutes.",
"The Moon always shows the same face to Earth. How many times does it turn against the stars in one trip around Earth?",
